---
import ProsePage from "../components/ProsePage.astro";
import { CONSENT_GRANTED, CONSENT_KEY } from "../consent/consent-store.ts";
import { PROVIDER_CHOICE_KEY } from "../scripts/provider-choice.ts";
import { PRIVACY_PAGE } from "../site/footer-pages.ts";
import {
  contactAddress,
  PRIVACY_CONTACT_EMAIL,
  SITE_HOST,
  SITE_MAINTAINER,
} from "../site/site-owner.ts";
import { GITHUB, GOOGLE_MAPS, MAP_PROVIDERS_SECTION, OPENFREEMAP } from "../site/third-parties.ts";

const contact = contactAddress(PRIVACY_CONTACT_EMAIL);
---

<ProsePage
  title={PRIVACY_PAGE.label}
  description="What maplacejs's site sends where, and what it keeps in your browser."
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">What this site sends where, and what it keeps in your browser.</Fragment>

  <section aria-labelledby="in-your-browser">
    <h2 id="in-your-browser">In your browser</h2>
    <p>
      This site's pages set no cookies of their own. Once you choose to load the maps, the site
      keeps one entry in your browser's local storage, <code>{CONSENT_KEY}</code> set to{" "}
      <code>{CONSENT_GRANTED}</code>, so later visits show the maps without asking again. Turning
      the maps off removes it. If your browser keeps no site data, your choice lasts for the page
      you made it on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choosing Google or MapLibre for the examples keeps one more entry, in session storage,{" "}
      <code>{PROVIDER_CHOICE_KEY}</code>, so the examples open on it again while the tab stays
      open; the browser drops it when the session ends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={MAP_PROVIDERS_SECTION}>
    <h2 id={MAP_PROVIDERS_SECTION}>The maps</h2>
    <p>
      The maps on this site come from two services:{" "}
      <a href={`#${GOOGLE_MAPS.id}`}>{GOOGLE_MAPS.name}</a> draws the examples you run on Google,
      and <a href={`#${OPENFREEMAP.id}`}>{OPENFREEMAP.name}</a> the map under the examples you run
      on MapLibre. Every map waits behind a placeholder, and nothing is asked of either until you
      choose “Load maps” on one of them or turn the maps on in the footer. That one choice covers
      both: from then on, each map loads as it scrolls into view, on this visit and later ones,
      until you turn the maps off. Pages without a map load neither.
    </p>
    <p>
      The maps switch in the footer of every page turns them off: it forgets your choice and
      reloads the page, since a loaded map can't be removed from an open page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={GOOGLE_MAPS.id}>
    <h2 id={GOOGLE_MAPS.id}>{GOOGLE_MAPS.name}</h2>
    <p>
      Once a map on Google loads, your browser fetches Google's Maps JavaScript API, then the
      map's tiles, images and fonts as you look around. With each request Google receives your IP
      address, what your browser says about itself (its user agent), the address of the page the
      map is on, and which part of the map you're viewing. The examples with a route also ask
      Google's Routes service for directions between their own stops. The site never asks for
      your location.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google may use its own cookies under its terms, and its Privacy Policy says what it does
      with what it receives:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {GOOGLE_MAPS.terms.map(({ label, url }) => <li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={OPENFREEMAP.id}>
    <h2 id={OPENFREEMAP.id}>{OPENFREEMAP.name}</h2>
    <p>
      Once a map on MapLibre loads, your browser fetches its style, tiles, fonts and icons from
      OpenFreeMap, at {OPENFREEMAP.domains.map((domain) => <code>{domain}</code>)}. With each
      request OpenFreeMap receives your IP address, what your browser says about itself (its user
      agent), the address of the page the map is on, and which part of the map you're viewing.
      OpenFreeMap may serve them through Cloudflare's network, which then receives the same
      requests under its own privacy policy (linked under <a href="#host">Who serves this site</a>).
      OpenFreeMap sets no cookies, and its Privacy Policy says what it keeps and for how long:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {OPENFREEMAP.terms.map(({ label, url }) => <li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby={GITHUB.id}>
    <h2 id={GITHUB.id}>{GITHUB.name}</h2>
    <p>
      The home page's star button shows how many people starred maplacejs on GitHub. To show it,
      your browser asks GitHub's API for the repository's count when the page opens, without
      cookies; with that request GitHub receives your IP address, your browser's user agent and
      this site's address. Its Privacy Statement says what it does with what it receives:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {GITHUB.terms.map(({ label, url }) => <li><a href={url}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="host">
    <h2 id="host">Who serves this site</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_HOST.name} serves this site's pages. As with any website, it receives each request
      your browser makes here: your IP address, your browser's user agent and the page asked for.
      To tell people from automated traffic, it may set a short-lived security cookie,{" "}
      <code>{SITE_HOST.securityCookie}</code>, which {SITE_HOST.name} keeps, not this site. Its{" "}
      <a href={SITE_HOST.privacyPolicy}>privacy policy</a> says what it does with what it receives.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="contact">
    <h2 id="contact">Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      maplacejs and this site are maintained by {SITE_MAINTAINER}. Questions about this page go to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProsePage>
